# Either, Two Versions #

# ^either is one of two, by a pair of buttons: a label and a splice #
# for each case, and a use means the case whose button is pressed. #
# It is a Macro whose code is fun a -> fun _ -> a (or the other), so #
# its splices reach the expansion as arguments (Sec. 3.2.5). #

# What should its Expansion be? It is whatever the two cases are -- #
# Ints in one use, Strings in another -- and a livelit declares ONE #
# expansion type (Sec. 2.3). The two slides that follow are the two #
# ways out: #

# 1. Unknown Expansion. Declare Expansion = ?, the unknown type. One #
#    definition serves every use, but a client gets ? from each, and #
#    checks its use of the result only gradually. #

# 2. Type Parameter. Write the definition as a type function, #
#    typfun A -> {... type Expansion = A ...}, and give each use its #
#    type through an abbreviation, let ^either_int = ^either@<Int>. #
#    Each use then has a static type, and a splice of the wrong type #
#    is an expansion error at the use. This goes beyond the paper, #
#    whose livelit parameters (Sec. 2.4.1) are values, not types; its #
#    abbreviations, let $uslider = $slider 0, are the model for the #
#    syntax. #

"see the two slides that follow"
